-- =====================================================================
-- Merge impact — OPTIONAL, ad-hoc (see docs/flink-collector.md 2.4).
--
-- Which merged records did a stuck trace feed? A merge reads the origin
-- stream, so a trace is counted in orders.flink_batched as soon as it is
-- placed, whether or not it ever finishes. This joins the stuck-trace
-- alerts to the merge edges on the one identity they share.
--
-- These are SELECTs for the session-cluster SQL Client, not a report:
-- IsotopeReportsJob does not run this file, and nothing here writes to a
-- topic. Paste them into `make flink-sql`, and stop them once you have your
-- answer: both are unbounded streaming joins, whose state grows until they
-- stop. Persisting either one would need its own sink, and the second emits
-- updates, so that sink could not be a plain append topic.
--
-- On CCAF, replace `stuck_trace_alerts_1m` with
-- `isotope_report_stuck_trace_1m`. The rest is the same on both runtimes.
--
-- Depends on:
--   - `stuck_trace_alerts_1m` (05_report_sinks.fql)
--   - `isotope_merge_edge_markers` (08_merge_provenance_sinks.fql), so the
--     reports must be running with merge provenance on
--
-- Reading the results:
--   - Impact means a merged record counted a trace that did not finish. The
--     order was still placed, so the merged record's figures are not wrong.
--   - An alert says the trace was stuck when its timer fired, not that it
--     still is. A trace can resume after alerting.
--   - The first query returns one row per alert, so a trace that alerts twice
--     appears twice. An alert with no row has no recorded edge: its origin hop
--     was dropped as late, or merge provenance was off during that window.
--   - The alert topic is read from the earliest offset, so it includes any
--     alerts written before StuckTracePTF stopped flagging finished traces.
-- =====================================================================

-- Per stuck trace: where it stopped, and the merged records it fed.
SELECT
    a.`trace_id`,
    a.`last_service`,
    a.`last_topic`,
    a.`last_seen_ts_ms`,
    e.`merge_trace_id`,
    e.`window_start`,
    e.`window_end`,
    e.`contributing_records`
FROM `stuck_trace_alerts_1m` AS a
JOIN `isotope_merge_edge_markers` AS e
    ON a.`trace_id` = e.`contributing_trace_id`;

-- Per merged record: how many of its traces have alerted.
--
-- COUNT(DISTINCT ...) on both sides, so repeat alerts and replayed edges cannot
-- inflate either figure. `contributing_traces` should equal the merged record's
-- distinct_traces. Keep the LEFT JOIN: without it, unaffected merged records
-- disappear and the total counts only the stuck traces.
SELECT
    e.`merge_trace_id`,
    e.`window_start`,
    e.`window_end`,
    COUNT(DISTINCT e.`contributing_trace_id`) AS `contributing_traces`,
    COUNT(DISTINCT a.`trace_id`)              AS `stuck_traces`
FROM `isotope_merge_edge_markers` AS e
LEFT JOIN `stuck_trace_alerts_1m` AS a
    ON e.`contributing_trace_id` = a.`trace_id`
GROUP BY
    e.`merge_trace_id`,
    e.`window_start`,
    e.`window_end`;
